# Template for ~/.seagrass-env on the Pi — the vehicle's entire configuration.
#
# Copy to the Pi as ~/.seagrass-env and fill in the values. Five systemd units
# read this one file via EnvironmentFile=: drone-server, media-uploader,
# renew-tls-cert, renew-turn-credentials, and anything started by `drone`.
#
# It is gitignored (.gitignore line 28) because it holds secrets, and it must
# stay that way. This template carries placeholders only — never paste a real
# token in here.
#
# BACK THE REAL FILE UP. It is the only thing on the Pi that is not
# reproducible from this repo: reflash the card and the code comes back with a
# git clone, but the token, the Supabase keys and the hardware paths do not.
# scripts/backup-pi-env.sh copies it somewhere safe.
#
# systemd's EnvironmentFile is NOT a shell. There is no `export`, no variable
# expansion, no `~`, and no command substitution. Paths must be absolute:
# `~/Sea-Grass-Drone/...` silently does not resolve, and the failure looks
# exactly like a missing file. Quotes are taken literally, so leave them off.
# Duplicate keys are allowed and the LAST one wins, which makes appending an
# override at the end a legitimate way to change a setting.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- identity ---

# Shared secret between this vehicle and the console. Must match the drone's
# "Access token" in the Fleet UI. Generated by running `drone` once.
SEAGRASS_TOKEN=replace-me

# Which drone in the fleet this is. Self-configured on first connect if unset.
#DRONE_ID=

# Supabase UIDs allowed to drive this vehicle, comma separated. Empty means the
# shared token above is the only credential.
#SEAGRASS_OWNER_UIDS=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- hardware ---

# Flight controller. The default is /dev/ttyACM0, which is a guess that is wrong
# as often as it is right — a Pixhawk can enumerate at ttyACM1 or ttyACM2, and
# the number changes between boots.
#
# Use the /dev/serial/by-id/ path instead. It is stable across reboots and names
# the board, so it survives the port shuffling that produces a "Pixhawk not
# available" message identical to a genuinely unplugged board. Find yours with:
#     ls -l /dev/serial/by-id/
# The Pixhawk exposes two interfaces; -if00 is MAVLink, -if02 is the console.
PIXHAWK_PORT=/dev/serial/by-id/usb-Holybro_Pixhawk6C_REPLACE-ME-if00
#PIXHAWK_BAUD=115200

# Blue Robotics Ping sonar. Ignored when the Ping is unpowered — the server says
# so explicitly at startup rather than failing quietly.
#PING_PORT=/dev/ttyAMA2
#PING_BAUD=115200

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- detector ---

# Absolute paths, both of them. Without these the detector runs stock COCO
# YOLOX-Nano — 80 land classes, useful only as a smoke test (point the camera at
# a person, expect a box).
#
# The model emits bare class indices and the labels file is the only thing that
# turns index 4 into `rov`. Ship one without the other and every detection is
# confidently mislabelled with nothing looking wrong. See training/README.md.
#DETECT_MODEL=/home/pi/Sea-Grass-Drone/server/vision/models/seagrass_nano.onnx
#DETECT_LABELS=/home/pi/Sea-Grass-Drone/server/vision/models/seagrass.txt

# Photograph what the detector finds on an unattended dive.
#DETECT_AUTOCAPTURE=0
#DETECT_AUTOCAPTURE_MIN_CONF=0.6
#DETECT_AUTOCAPTURE_COOLDOWN_S=15

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ camera ---

# The Pi 5 has NO hardware H.264 encoder, so this is software x264 and the
# resolution and frame rate are real CPU cost. Lower both before reaching for
# anything else if the feed lags. Halving to 960x540 roughly halves the work.
#CAM_WIDTH=1280
#CAM_HEIGHT=720
#CAM_FPS=30
#CAM_BITRATE=2000

# Colour boost on the operator's view. Costs two CPU filters. Turning it off
# does NOT affect detection: the detector's frame is teed off ahead of these and
# it runs its own, stronger correction.
#CAM_UNDERWATER=1

# ---------------------------------------------------------- remote access ----

# Driving the vehicle from an https:// page needs a real wss://, so these are
# required for remote control and irrelevant on a LAN.
#TLS_HOSTNAME=
#WS_TLS_CERT=
#WS_TLS_KEY=

# Cloudflare TURN, renewed by scripts/renew-turn-credentials.py.
#TURN_KEY_ID=
#TURN_KEY_API_TOKEN=

# ------------------------------------------------------------ media upload ---

# Used by media-uploader.service to push captures to Supabase Storage. The
# SERVICE key, not the anon key — it bypasses RLS, so it is the most dangerous
# value in this file and the best reason to keep it out of git.
#SUPABASE_URL=
#SUPABASE_SERVICE_KEY=
#SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET=
#SUPABASE_BUCKET=

# Everything else — control feel, sonar braking, heading hold — has a working
# default in the code and is documented in SETUP.md. Set here only what you have
# deliberately changed, so this file stays a list of decisions rather than a
# copy of the defaults.
